1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,173
Exact equations come from a really elegant idea.

2
00:00:04,173 --> 00:00:06,687
The answer is not a formula for y.

3
00:00:06,687 --> 00:00:10,795
It is a level curve, of some hidden function of x and y.

4
00:00:11,515 --> 00:00:14,728
Picture a function f of x and y as a landscape.

5
00:00:14,728 --> 00:00:20,023
Every curve where f equals a constant, is like a contour line on a hiking map.

6
00:00:20,443 --> 00:00:24,669
Walk along one contour line, and your height never changes.

7
00:00:24,669 --> 00:00:30,284
That is exactly what the equation M dx, plus N dy, equals zero, is saying.

8
00:00:34,084 --> 00:00:35,724
Here is the link.

9
00:00:35,724 --> 00:00:41,738
Move a tiny bit in x and in y, and the change in f is the total differential.

10
00:00:41,738 --> 00:00:44,608
d f equals f x dx, plus f y dy.

11
00:00:45,029 --> 00:00:48,935
Now compare with M dx, plus N dy, equals zero.

12
00:00:48,935 --> 00:00:55,083
If M is f x, and N is f y, then the equation is just saying, d f equals zero.

13
00:00:55,502 --> 00:00:58,815
And d f equals zero means f never changes.

14
00:00:58,815 --> 00:01:02,127
So the solution is f of x y, equals c.

15
00:01:02,547 --> 00:01:08,100
But how do you know such an f even exists? Mixed partial derivatives.

16
00:01:08,100 --> 00:01:11,578
If M is f x and N is f y, then M sub y and N sub

17
00:01:11,578 --> 00:01:14,990
x are both f x y, so they must be equal.

18
00:01:15,411 --> 00:01:18,505
That is your exactness test from Lecture 4.

19
00:01:18,505 --> 00:01:22,157
Partial M partial y, equals partial N partial x.

20
00:01:26,056 --> 00:01:28,673
Lecture 4 turns that into a short method.

21
00:01:29,093 --> 00:01:32,186
One. Check that M sub y equals N sub x.

22
00:01:32,607 --> 00:01:37,056
Two. Integrate M with respect to x, holding y constant.

23
00:01:37,056 --> 00:01:41,382
Instead of plus C, add a function g of y, because anything that

24
00:01:41,382 --> 00:01:45,587
depends only on y, disappears when you differentiate in x.

25
00:01:46,007 --> 00:01:50,585
Three. Differentiate that result with respect to y, and set it equal to N.

26
00:01:51,005 --> 00:01:54,693
Four. Solve for g prime of y. Every x has to cancel.

27
00:01:54,693 --> 00:01:57,624
If an x survives, something went wrong earlier.

28
00:01:58,045 --> 00:02:03,404
Five. Integrate to get g of y, and write the answer as f of x y, equals c.

29
00:02:07,103 --> 00:02:09,890
Let us do Example 1 from Lecture 4.

30
00:02:09,890 --> 00:02:14,734
Two x y dx, plus x squared minus one, dy, equals zero.

31
00:02:15,154 --> 00:02:18,326
M is two x y. N is x squared minus one.

32
00:02:18,746 --> 00:02:24,927
Test it. M sub y is two x. N sub x is also two x. They match, so it is exact.

33
00:02:25,347 --> 00:02:27,669
Integrate M with respect to x.

34
00:02:27,669 --> 00:02:30,492
f equals x squared y, plus g of y.

35
00:02:30,912 --> 00:02:35,848
Differentiate in y. f sub y equals x squared plus g prime of y.

36
00:02:35,848 --> 00:02:39,182
Set that equal to N, x squared minus one.

37
00:02:39,602 --> 00:02:46,011
The x squared cancels. g prime of y equals negative one, so g of y is negative y.

38
00:02:46,431 --> 00:02:49,765
So f is x squared y minus y, and the solution is

39
00:02:49,765 --> 00:02:53,741
the level curve, x squared y minus y, equals c.

40
00:02:54,161 --> 00:02:56,145
Here we can even solve for y.

41
00:02:56,145 --> 00:02:58,514
y equals c over x squared minus one.

42
00:02:58,934 --> 00:03:02,497
And those are the contour lines from the start of the video.

43
00:03:02,497 --> 00:03:05,102
Every value of c gives one solution curve.

44
00:03:09,402 --> 00:03:10,729
Three traps to avoid.

45
00:03:11,149 --> 00:03:16,036
One. Writing plus C, instead of plus g of y, after you integrate M.

46
00:03:16,036 --> 00:03:19,275
That throws away exactly the piece you need to find.

47
00:03:19,695 --> 00:03:22,355
Two. An x left over in g prime of y.

48
00:03:22,355 --> 00:03:26,344
If that happens, stop, and recheck your partial derivatives.

49
00:03:26,764 --> 00:03:29,203
Three. Forgetting the equals c.

50
00:03:29,203 --> 00:03:34,027
The answer is the level curve, f of x y equals c, not just the function f.

51
00:03:37,828 --> 00:03:40,658
So here is the recipe to take into the midterm.

52
00:03:41,078 --> 00:03:45,708
One. Test M sub y equals N sub x, and write that it is exact.

53
00:03:46,128 --> 00:03:48,807
Two. Integrate M in x, plus g of y.

54
00:03:49,226 --> 00:03:54,402
Three. Set f sub y equal to N, and solve for g prime. No x allowed.

55
00:03:54,822 --> 00:03:58,844
Four. Integrate g prime, and write f of x y equals c.

56
00:03:59,264 --> 00:04:02,315
Next up, what to do when the exactness test fails.

57
00:04:02,315 --> 00:04:04,215
The special integrating factor.
